sec.p:
Among the available cantilever calibration methods, the thermal noise-based approach is widely used due to its firm theoretical foundation, non-destructive nature, applicability across a wide range of cantilever stiffnesses and environmental conditions, and the fact that the fitting procedure itself requires no prior knowledge of cantilever geometry [16–19]. It is particularly relevant to high-time-resolution measurements of biomolecules using ultrashort cantilevers or cantilevers with modified geometries, since the alternative method of Sader et al [20–22] requires precise knowledge of the cantilever’s plan dimensions that become difficult to measure in these cases. The thermal method is grounded in the equipartition theorem, which assigns to each quadratic degree of freedom of a thermal system an average energy of \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
