\begin{equation*}{S_{xx}}\left(f\right) = \frac{2{k_\mathrm{B}}T}{\pi k{f_0}Q}\cdot \frac 1{{\left[ {\left( \frac f{{f_0}}\right) ^2}-1\right] ^2}+{\left( \frac f{{f_0}Q}\right) ^2}}+{S_\mathrm{w}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}Sxx(f)=2kBTπkf0Q⋅1[(ff0)2−1]2+(ff0Q)2+Sw.

Thermal displacement PSDs were generated using the Sader-Bellon model, which describes a cantilever as a continuous elastic beam, clamped at one end and free at the other, immersed in a viscous fluid. The Sader-Bellon model assumes a uniform rectangular beam that is long relative to its width, so the simulated PSDs represent cantilevers that satisfy these geometric assumptions. Commercial probes may depart from this idealization in their shape, dimensional uniformity, or the additional mass contributed by the tip. These assumptions pertain to the generation of the model PSDs and are distinct from the subsequent approximation of fitting those PSDs with the simple harmonic oscillator (SHO) model, whose accuracy is evaluated separately in this work. The PSD contribution of flexural mode n is given by: [25]
