In addition to differences in weighting and the inclusion of Sw, we used both local (i.e. confined to a frequency range in the vicinity of the resonant peak) and global fitting strategies. In the local strategy, the fit window spanned \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
$\left[ \frac{{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}}{\alpha},{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}\times \alpha \right]$\end{document}[fpeakα,fpeak×α], where the window half-width α sets how much of the spectrum on either side of the peak is included and fpeak is the numerical maximum of the first mode of the spectrum; α was varied systematically to find the optimum value (α = 3.5 for the noiseless spectra and α = 2.5 once a detection-noise floor was included; see sections 3.5 and 3.7). Because the apparent PSD peak can differ substantially from the f0 value identified from the dynamic-stiffness analysis (above) under strong damping conditions, the local fit was performed iteratively. After each fit, the window was recentered on the newly fitted f0 rather than on fpeak and the fit was repeated. We found that five iterations were always sufficient for convergence. In the global fit, a single fit was performed over a large fixed range from 10 Hz to 20 times the second-mode resonance frequency.

The accuracy of each fit was quantified as the percent bias in the recovered k relative to the input value used to generate the PSD, with positive values indicating overestimation of k:
