$\left[ \frac{{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}}{\alpha},{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}\times \alpha \right]$\end{document}[fpeakα,fpeak×α], with α = 3.5) recovered the input k with low bias across all four media tested, for both cantilevers. For the stiff cantilever (BL-AC40TS), the local-fit k bias was +2.4% in air, +1.9% in water, +1.9% in 4 M NaH2PO4, and −0.5% in 75% glycerol (table 1; figure 3). For the soft cantilever (MLCT-Bio B), the corresponding biases were +2.3%, +2.4%, +0.3%, and −2.1% (table 1; figure S2). In both cases, the bias remained below roughly 2.4% in magnitude even as the quality factor of the fundamental mode fell from Q ≈ 18–27 in air to as low as Q ≈ 0.4 in glycerol (i.e. overdamped). The Sader-Bellon PSD departs from the SHO shape in every medium, lacking the flat low-frequency plateau and the f−4 rolloff that the SHO assumes. What changes with increasing viscosity is how much of the spectrum lies in these mismatched regions relative to the resonance itself (figure 3).

