$\left[ \frac{{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}}{3.5},{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}\times 3.5\right]$\end{document}[fpeak3.5,fpeak×3.5] and recentering on the recovered f0 over five iterations; the global fit was a single fit spanning the full spectrum. The gray dotted vertical line marks the fluid-loaded resonance frequency f0 of the fundamental mode. Note that each fit is drawn only over the frequency range on which it was performed. The local fit would also depart from the model PSD if extrapolated to a wider frequency range.

sec.p:
These results bear directly on the conclusions of Pirzer and Hugel, who found experimentally that thermal calibration in 75% glycerol deviated by up to 50% from that in water and concluded that the method was unreliable in media of high viscosity [28]. Their fits extended from the lowest sampled frequency to the minimum between the first two flexural modes, and the recovered k was obtained by integrating the fitted function to give \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
$\langle {x^2}\rangle$\end{document}⟨x2⟩. Both features make the result sensitive to the off-resonance regions where the SHO lineshape departs most strongly from the true response. In fact, their analysis showed the recovered k varied by more than 50% as the lower limit of the fit range was moved. Our global-fitting results similarly show that large biases can be obtained under certain fitting conditions (table 1), but also that restricting the fit to a window around the resonance and recentering that window iteratively removes most of that bias. We note that the comparison with Pirzer and Hugel is not exact, since their values were obtained from experimental spectra and include additional contributions from optical lever sensitivity and noise.

The iterative local fitting results used a window half-width α = 3.5, spanning \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
$\left[ \frac{{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}}{3.5},{f_{\mathrm{peak}}}\times 3.5\right]$\end{document}[fpeak3.5,fpeak×3.5] around the recentered peak. To assess the sensitivity of the results to this choice, we carried out the iterative local fit for the stiff cantilever (BL-AC40TS) across values of α = 1.5–5.0 in steps of 0.5 in all four media. In air, absolute δk was essentially insensitive to α, remaining near 2.3% across the entire range. In the three liquid media, however, the absolute bias traced out a clear U-shaped dependence on α. Bias was large at small α—up to 17% at α = 1.5 in glycerol—passed through a minimum whose location shifted to larger α with increasing viscosity (near α = 2.5 in water, 3.0 in 4 M NaH2PO4, and 3.5 in 75% glycerol), and rose again at larger α, reaching 3%–5% by α = 5.0 (figure 5). At α = 3.5, the bias remained below 3% in all media.
